      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-104343",
        "knowledge_point_id": "4ed5facf-f654-5890-974f-bbdcd9641515",
        "knowledge_point": "Applying De Moivre's Theorem to Fractions",
        "problem": "Simplify the expression $\\frac{6}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ]^{5}}.$",
        "worked_solution": "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ]^{5}}=[\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ]^{-5}.\n$$\n\nDe Moivre's Theorem states that\n\n$$\n[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi ]^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=-5$ and $\\phi =3\\theta .$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ]^{5}} & =6[\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ]^{-5} \\\\  & =6[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(-5)\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(-5)\\theta )] \\\\  & =6[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-15\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-15\\theta )].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\phi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\phi )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )$ for any input $\\phi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n6[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-15\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-15\\theta )]=6[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(15\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(15\\theta )].\n$$",
