      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-116618",
        "knowledge_point_id": "903cd6d7-18f2-5b53-a8e9-9aaef5589549",
        "knowledge_point": "Using the Trapezoidal Rule: Word Problem",
        "problem": "Lisa is selling orange juice. The following table shows the rate, in liters per hour, at which it is sold.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hour})$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $c(t)\\,(\\text{lts/hour})$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n\nUse the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[1,2]\\cup [2,5],\n$$\n\nto approximate the total number of liters of juice sold by Lisa over a 4 hours period.",
        "worked_solution": "In this context, we have $2$ trapezoids, and the area $A$ under the curve $y=c(t)$ represents the total number of liters sold over a $4$ period.\n\nUsing the trapezoidal rule, we estimate the area as follows:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{c(1)+c(2)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{1}+\\frac{c(2)+c(5)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{3+5}{2}\\cdot (2-1)+\\frac{5+7}{2}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =22\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Lisa sold approximately $22\\,\\text{liters}$ over a $4$ hours period.",
