      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-152231",
        "knowledge_point_id": "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279",
        "knowledge_point": "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions",
        "problem": "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
        "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{5}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{2\\cdot {5}^{x/2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x/2}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{5}^{a/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x/2}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x/2}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x/2}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056962/assets/141d1745dc939ca63155ca3313257f385af6d3c505c16bd63ba3b91e1e0ba64b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
